 are e3407a134a02cf1f4942812b26a55652f15665b347af02a4eeda78980a1a479f, bound by run.json\"\n59\t  - path: .gnd-planning/milestones/ossie-relational-bundle/notes/reviews/phase-OB1-r1/SANITIZATION.md\n60\t    sha256: 8dccb00b735218b490fbe8ca100156ed9b1c2aebcffc8d06eec2a0ba0447ff4e\n61\t  - path: .gnd-planning/milestones/ossie-relational-bundle/notes/reviews/phase-OB1-r1/FINDINGS.md\n62\t    sha256: 03dad4871dae51f76dd8cec0636931cbb5135b4182473befdee668145e493644\n63\t---\n64\t\n65\t# Phase OB1: parameters for the Ossie size bounds\n66\t\n67\t## Claim\n68\t\n69\t- **Export flag.** `gnd export ossie` takes `--max-output-bytes N`, which\n70\t  bounds each written artifact. The default is 10485760 bytes and the\n71\t  ceiling 268435456.\n72\t- **Unchanged default.** Without the flag, every census export is\n73\t  byte-identical to the baseline at `1926e895`: 82 entries, 41 models in two\n74\t  formats (T1).\n75\t- **Large exports.** The generated 5,000-fact model has a 10.95 MB loss\n76\t  report. It publishes with `--max-output-bytes 33554432` and is refused at\n77\t  the default with `ossie:output-size` (T2, T2b).\n78\t- **Each artifact bounded.** Whichever artifact is largest, the run refuses\n79\t  at that size minus 1 and publishes at that size (T3, T3a). A strict\n80\t  refusal still precedes the size refusal (T3b).\n81\t- **Import flag** (D3 b). `gnd import ossie` takes `--max-input-bytes N`.\n82\t  - It moves the read cap, the YAML guard, both JSON decoder caps and the\n83\t    loss-size cap (2N).\n84\t  - The model-size cap stays fixed (T6, T6b, T6c).\n85\t  - The large bundle round-trips in JSON and YAML to a model equal to its\n86\t    source (T6).\n87\t- **Invalid bounds.** An invalid N exits 64 for either flag before any read\n88\t  (T4, T6d). The library functions refuse one with `ValueError`.\n89\t- **Docs.** The normative paragraph in `representation-format.md` §\n90\t  \"Ossie interchange\" states the flags, the default, the ceiling, exit 64\n91\t  and the kept cap.\n92\t\n93\t## Gate\n94\t\n95\tThe front matter gives each command and its exit verbatim.\n96\t\n97\t**Known red.** `tests/test_development_scaffold.py::test_reference_closure_and_declared_bootstrap`\n98\tfails on the phase base. This diff was set aside with\n99\t`git stash push -u -m ob1-knownred-check-7b24`, and the test ran on base\n100\t`a7847f2b`:\n101\t\n102\t    FAILED tests/test_development_scaffold.py::test_reference_closure_and_declared_bootstrap\n103\t    1 failed in 0.47s\n104\t\n105\tThe stash was then applied by SHA and dropped. The test checks that\n106\t`.agents/skills/review-pr/SKILL.md` resolves `../process-phase/references/review-tool.md`.\n107\tThat is a discovery-link layout this worktree has not adopted (AGENTS.md:\n108\t`uv run gnd install --symlink --project --force`), and OB1 touches no part\n109\tof it.\n110\t\n111\t## Review\n112\t\n113\tThere was one reviewer, Codex, run through `review_tool.py` with a read-only\n114\tsandbox and effort high. **One reviewer is one reviewer.**\n115\t\n116\t| Item | Value |\n117\t|---|---|\n118\t| Brief sha256 | `f14e9e6c…6083` |\n119\t| Review | [review.md](notes/reviews/phase-OB1-r1/review.md), sha256 `e3407a13…479f` as written, `f8272abf…8c92` after path sanitization ([SANITIZATION.md](notes/reviews/phase-OB1-r1/SANITIZATION.md)) |\n120\t| Verdict | CHANGES_REQUESTED, with two findings |\n121\t| Ledger | [FINDINGS.md](notes/reviews/phase-OB1-r1/FINDINGS.md) |\n122\t\n123\t| Id | Class | Reproduced | Disposition |\n124\t|---|---|---|---|\n125\t| F1 | blocking | yes: 4 RF7 controls failed | **fixed.** Defaults resolve `MAX_BYTES` per call. The four existing RF7 tests are the regressions, shown failing on the pre-fix bytes. |\n126\t| F2 | completeness | yes | **fixed** for the normative paragraph. Rejected for the help text: help states defaults and is not a normative home. |\n127\t\n128\tThe reviewer independently confirmed several claims:\n129\t- All 82 baseline entries match.\n130\t- Each artifact is bounded at size minus 1, and the strict refusal comes\n131\t  first.\n132\t- An invalid bound exits 64 with no model read, including a 5,000-digit\n133\t  integer.\n134\t- Large JSON and YAML bundles round-trip, and the loss-size and model-size\n135\t  behaviour is as claimed.\n136\t- The 10 MiB defaults are exact.\n137\t\n138\tIts standing matcher probes found no input the code mishandles.\n139\t\n140\t**The repaired bytes are not reviewed.** These are F1, F2 and the\n141\trefactoring below, which came after the review. No second round ran.\n142\t\n143\t## Refactoring review\n144\t\n145\t**Refactor EXECUTED.**\n146\t- **What:** `render_ossie` and `plan_ossie_import` each validated their bound\n147\t  with a copy of the same check. Both now call\n148\t  `input_policy.require_byte_bound(name, value)`.\n149\t- **Behaviour:** preserved. The messages still name the parameter.\n150\t- **Tests:** `test_ob1_library_bound_is_validated` covers the export side,\n151\t  and the new `test_ob1_library_input_bound_is_validated` covers the import\n152\t  side.\n153\t- **Gate:** the same focused gate and guard block reran green apart from the\n154\t  known red.\n155\t\n156\t**Seen and deferred.**\n157\t- **The defect:** `input_policy.guard_yaml` still captures `MAX_BYTES` in its\n158\t  default argument at definition time. This is the class F1 found in the\n159\t  code OB1 added, but it predates OB1 and OB1 does not change it. A test that\n160\t  lowers `MAX_BYTES` does not lower `guard_yaml`'s default.\n161\t- **Where it goes:** an open-questions row in this milestone (below), for\n162\t  the OB5 or close sweep.\n163\t\n164\t## Refuted premises and findings\n165\t\n166\tThe spec's §12 lists IF1–IF9. The ones that change what an earlier document\n167\tsays:\n168\t\n169\t- **IF1, a refuted premise.** Plan r2 §1.2 says no test covered\n170\t  `ossie:output-size`. One did, in `tests/test_ossie_import.py`.\n171\t- **IF2.** The plan named five import caps. There were six: `JSONDecoder`\n172\t  re-checks the cap after `loads`.\n173\t- **IF3.** Spec T6b is unreachable for the YAML guard and the JSON decode\n174\t  caps, because they equal the read cap over the same bytes.\n175\t- **IF7.** The plan's §9 affected-consumer list missed the CLI pins and the\n176\t  RF7 input-policy tests.\n177\t- **IF9, for D11.** The measured output-to-source ratio is about 6×. A\n178\t  32 MiB export needs a source of only about 5.3 MB, so D11 probably needs\n179\t  no work. This is an estimate, pending the reporter's model size (D10).\n180\t\n181\t## Open questions\n182\t\n183\t| Id | Question | Target |\n184\t|---|---|---|\n185\t| OB1-Q1 | Should `guard_yaml`'s default `max_bytes` resolve `MAX_BYTES` at call time, like `loads` now does? | the OB5 or milestone-close sweep |\n186\t| OB1-Q2 | D11: is a source-cap parameter needed? IF9 suggests not. | after D10's answer |\n187\t\n188\t## Boundary\n189\t\n190\t- **Size** (spec §10 estimated about 70 source and 220 test lines):\n191\t  - Source: 121 lines added and 38 removed across six modules. About 1.7×\n192\t    the estimate, because of the decoder cap (IF2), the per-call defaults\n193\t    (F1) and the shared check.\n194\t  - Tests: 256 lines in test files, plus a 48-line support module and the\n195\t    780-line baseline data file.\n196\t- **Not done:** no semantic model, mapping or Rmap (OB2–OB4); no streaming;\n197\t  no change to `MAX_BYTES` or `MAX_MODEL_BYTES`.\n198\t- **Not run:** the opt-in oracle lane, and the evidence and census lanes.\n199\t- **Approval:** this record approves nothing beyond OB1. Merge is the\n200\t  owner's."}
