f3c48684fd8e76382e27\n79\t---\n80\t\n81\t# Phase OB4: the physical binding (`--physical-binding FILE`)\n82\t\n83\t## Claim\n84\t\n85\t- **The flag.** `gnd export ossie MODEL --out OUT --physical-binding FILE`\n86\t  writes the same five files. The semantic model describes the caller's\n87\t  delivered tables, and the mapping maps the ontology onto them. The\n88\t  built-in Rmap is not run (T6).\n89\t- **The approved corpus holds.** All seven valid cases equal their\n90\t  hand-written files byte for byte, and all 50 bad bindings are refused by\n91\t  their rule id with exit 3 and nothing published (T1, T4).\n92\t- **Every member is consumed** (OB4-C1): tables, columns, `sql_type`,\n93\t  `targets`, `references`, `primary_key`, `unique_keys` present and absent,\n94\t  `roles` and `extension_roles` each have a case.\n95\t- **One relationship per foreign key** (OB4-C7): a two-column foreign key is\n96\t  one relationship with ordered columns; a mismatched group is refused (T3).\n97\t- **One order** (OB4-C2, C3; plan §0 rule 3). Nineteen rule ids are checked\n98\t  in the order the source states them. 152 pairs of defects each report the\n99\t  earlier rule; binding syntax and schema come before the model, names and\n100\t  contradictions after it, and all before `--strict` and size (T5).\n101\t- **Unbound is not an error** (OB4-C4). An unbound fact type and its\n102\t  constraints are listed as `unbound`, with `binding_sha256` the digest of\n103\t  the binding file (T6).\n104\t- **Without the flag nothing changes** (OB4-C5). The 82 census exports equal\n105\t  a baseline captured from main before any OB4 edit, and the OB2 and OB3\n106\t  corpora still pass (T7). With the flag, the ontology, sidecar and loss\n107\t  report are the bytes of a run without it (T2).\n108\t- **Total over hostile input** (OB4-C6; plan §0 rule 1). 910 wrong values in\n109\t  a valid binding, 14 byte strings, lone surrogates and a 1,100-entity\n110\t  identifier chain give only named binding refusals or a valid run, never a\n111\t  traceback (T8, R1-F1, R1-F2). Role names match by the D8 rule, including\n112\t  compatibility forms; names that collide or vanish are refused (T9).\n113\t- **Time** (plan §0 rule 2). At 1,400 entities and 4,500 fact types (2,950\n114\t  tables) a binding run takes 4.1 s, against 3.4 s with the Rmap; the ratio\n115\t  to one fifth of that scale is 5.0. The binding file is 1.2 MB (T10).\n116\t- **One walk** (plan §0 rule 4). The binding path fills the Rmap's own\n117\t  records, and `gnd.rmap.dispositions` performs the walk for both paths.\n118\t- **One home.** The rules are in `representation-format.md` § \"Ossie\n119\t  interchange\", \"Physical binding\"; the member list is the packaged schema.\n120\t\n121\t## Gate\n122\t\n123\tThe front matter gives each command and its exit verbatim.\n124\t\n125\t**Known red.** `tests/test_development_scaffold.py::test_reference_closure_and_declared_bootstrap`\n126\tfails because `.agents/skills/review-pr/SKILL.md` cannot resolve\n127\t`../process-phase/references/review-tool.md`, a discovery-link layout this\n128\tworktree has not adopted. It failed on the phase base: main at `165fa7c7`\n129\thas the tree `5718e427`, which is the tree OB3's gate ran on\n130\t(`1 failed, 865 passed`, that test being the failure). OB4 touches no part\n131\tof that layout.\n132\t\n133\t## Review\n134\t\n135\tThere was one reviewer, Codex, run through `review_tool.py` with a read-only\n136\tsandbox and effort high, over base `ba192972` to head `a8a41130`. **One\n137\treviewer is one reviewer.**\n138\t\n139\t| Item | Value |\n140\t|---|---|\n141\t| Review | [review.md](notes/reviews/phase-OB4-r1/review.md), sha256 `88049c0c…560a` as written, `c9c596d7…f6e1` after sanitization ([SANITIZATION.md](notes/reviews/phase-OB4-r1/SANITIZATION.md)) |\n142\t| Verdict | CHANGES_REQUESTED, with six findings |\n143\t| Ledger | [FINDINGS.md](notes/reviews/phase-OB4-r1/FINDINGS.md) |\n144\t\n145\t| Id | Class | Reproduced | Disposition |\n146\t|---|---|---|---|\n147\t| F1 | blocking | yes (exit 70) | fixed: a string that is not Unicode text is `binding:syntax` |\n148\t| F2 | blocking | yes (`RecursionError`) | fixed: explicit stack, and an identifier nested more than 8 deep is not followed |\n149\t| F3 | blocking | yes | fixed: an external uniqueness is placed by the roles it names |\n150\t| F4 | hardening | yes | fixed: foreign-key groups are checked in sorted order |\n151\t| F5 | hardening | yes | fixed: a word after the type's list needs a space |\n152\t| F6 | hardening | yes | fixed: schema refusals name an escaped pointer |\n153\t\n154\tEleven regression tests were shown failing on the pre-repair bytes before\n155\tany fix. The repairs are commit `527cf956`.\n156\t\n157\tThe reviewer also confirmed: 149 tests over the approved outputs, schemas\n158\tand baseline; all 50 refusal rows; all 910 wrong-shape probes; all five\n159\tartifact digests equal to `ba192972` across the 82 baseline runs; the\n160\tgenerated binding runs listing every fact type and constraint without\n161\tinvoking the Rmap; and its matcher probes of dotted, quoted, Unicode and\n162\tnewline-containing identifiers, role-name normalization and SQL parameter\n163\tboundaries, where the only failure was F5.\n164\t\n165\t**The repaired bytes are not reviewed.** No second round ran.\n166\t\n167\t## Refactoring review\n168\t\n169\t**Refactors EXECUTED**, behind the same gates and with no OB2 or OB3 corpus\n170\tbyte changed:\n171\t- **The foreign-key naming rule has one home.** It was inline in the Rmap's\n172\t  build. It is now `gnd.rmap.name_foreign_keys`, used by the Rmap and the\n173\t  binding.\n174\t- **The unary flag is a field, not a convention.** The mapping read a unary\n175\t  fact type's flag column out of the position of a name in `carried_as`.\n176\t  `RolePlacement.flag` now carries it, and the binding path leaves it unset.\n177\t- **Absorption is a field.** The mapping inferred an absorbed subtype from a\n178\t  table name that differed from the object type id, which a binding breaks.\n179\t  `Identification.absorbed` states it.\n180\t- **One fixture-model scan** (OB3-Q6): `tests/support/model_scan.py`.\n181\t\n182\t**Seen and deferred.**\n183\t- An RFC 6901 pointer is now built by four private helpers\n184\t  (`export/ossie.py`, `ossie_import.py`, `transition.py` in its own style,\n185\t  and the binding). Row OB4-Q4.\n186\t- OB3's `MAX_LINK_ARITY` and OB4's `MAX_IDENTIFIER_DEPTH` are two bounds on\n187\t  one concern, the depth of a mapping document. Row OB4-Q5.\n188\t\n189\t## Refuted premises and findings\n190\t\n191\tThe spec's §12 (IF1–IF14), §13 (K1–K13) and §14 (RR1–RR6) list them all. The\n192\tones that change an earlier document:\n193\t\n194\t- **Plan §2.4** says a foreign key's columns are \"ordered by each column's\n195\t  position in `references.columns`\". Every column of a group carries the\n196\t  same block, so no column has a position there. They pair by table column\n197\t  order (K1).\n198\t- **Plan §10 D8** names four contradiction classes without definitions. Two\n199\t  needed one before they could be tested (K4, K5), and K5 as first written\n200\t  refused the approved case V3 (IF1).\n201\t- **Plan §2.4** treats `targets` as how a column gets its meaning. `roles`\n202\t  alone binds a role; `identifier` targets mark home tables, and the other\n203\t  two kinds are only checked (K12).\n204\t- **Spec §6.7** put `binding:role-name` after the unknown-role lookup. It\n205\t  has to come before (K2).\n206\t- **Spec §6.5** expanded an entity's identifier \"in turn\" without a bound.\n207\t  A chain the model admits can be over a thousand deep (RR2).\n208\t- **The issue** asks that the datasets equal a delivered DDL. A schema that\n209\t  links tables by surrogate keys can be described, but the fact types held\n210\t  only through those keys cannot be bound (K13).\n211\t\n212\tThe plan is not edited here; these go to the milestone-close amendment.\n213\t\n214\t## Open questions\n215\t\n216\t| Id | Question | Target |\n217\t|---|---|---|\n218\t| OB4-Q1 | Accept the two rules implementation and review added: K5's refinement (an entity and a part of its own identifier may share a column, IF1) and the 8-level identifier depth limit (RR2)? | owner, before OB5 |\n219\t| OB4-Q2 | Should the review repairs (`527cf956`) get a second round, or is the close review the next instrument? | owner |\n220\t| OB4-Q3 | **Surrogate keys (K13).** Does the reporter's schema use them? If so, most of their fact types may be unbindable as specified. | D10 |\n221\t| OB4-Q4 | One shared RFC 6901 pointer helper for the four private ones? | close sweep |\n222\t| OB4-Q5 | One depth bound for mapping documents instead of two (OB3's 32 roles, OB4's 8 identifier levels)? | close sweep |\n223\t| OB4-Q6 | OB2's `test_r2_f8_external_unique_keys_are_not_quadratic` failed once in a long run and passed three times alone (IF14). Loosen its ratio, or leave it? | close sweep |\n224\t| OB3-Q3 | Carried: the opt-in oracle lane (OB3 T11, OB4 T13) has not run, so upstream acceptance of these files is unconfirmed. | owner |\n225\t| OB3-Q5 | Carried: identifiers the ontology does not declare (unit-based, multi-supertype). | close sweep |\n226\t| OB1-Q1, OB1-Q2 | Carried: `guard_yaml`'s captured default; D11, the source cap. | OB5 or the close sweep; after D10 |\n227\t| OB2-Q1, OB2-Q2 | Carried: the duplicated predicate; `refscheme`'s selection. | close sweep |\n228\t\n229\t## Boundary\n230\t\n231\t- **Residuals.**\n232\t  - **Surrogate keys (K13)**, accepted at the corpus gate as a known limit.\n233\t  - **The reporter's format is unconfirmed (G1).** The document is GND's\n234\t    own, defined from the issue's prose. D10 has not been asked.\n235\t  - **`extension_roles` has no confirmed meaning (G7).** It is checked and\n236\t    listed, and nothing is mapped from it.\n237\t  - **Lowercase-folding databases (G11).** Names are quoted and uppercased in\n238\t    expressions, by Ossie's comparison rule.\n239\t  - **No nullability.** A binding carries none, so `mandatory` is always\n240\t    `not-relational` on a binding run.\n241\t- **Size** (spec §10 estimated about 450 reader, 120 schema, 60 wiring, 110\n242\t  normative and 500 test lines):\n243\t  - `physical_binding.py`: 722 lines, about 1.6× the estimate, from the\n244\t    declared-home and object-mapping distinction (IF2), the stack-based\n245\t    identifier shape (RR2) and the per-rule sorted passes.\n246\t  - The schema file: 194 lines.\n247\t  - `rmap.py`, `ossie.py`, `ossie_relational.py`, `commands/export.py`: 117\n248\t    lines added and 36 removed, about 2× the estimate, from the shared-record\n249\t    generalization (IF7).\n250\t  - Normative text: 115 lines.\n251\t  - Tests: `test_physical_binding.py` 787 lines, the corpus checker 250 and\n252\t    the binding generator 35, about 2× the estimate.\n253\t  - Corpus: 7 valid cases and 50 refusal rows.\n254\t- **Not done:** the explanation, how-to, export skill and release-note entry\n255\t  (OB5): **they still describe three files and no binding.** No binding from\n256\t  DDL, no DDL from a binding, no YAML binding, no size parameter.\n257\t- **Not run:** the opt-in oracle lane (T13), and the evidence and census\n258\t  lanes.\n259\t- **Not pushed.** Branch `claude/ossie-ob4-binding-spec`, cut from main at\n260\t  `165fa7c7`, has no pull request; opening one is the owner's call.\n261\t- **Approval:** this record approves nothing beyond OB4. Merge is the\n262\t  owner's."}
