files`, and mentions in the README, the\n87\t   index page and the CLI reference.\n88\t2. **Two links broken on main** were fixed on the owner's word (018), so the\n89\t   strict build passes.\n90\t3. **The oracle lane ran for the first time** over the relational files\n91\t   (018, H7). It found a defect in merged OB3 and OB4 code, which was\n92\t   repaired here on the owner's ruling (019).\n93\t\n94\t## The oracle lane and the quoting fix\n95\t\n96\tThe pinned upstream validator accepted the ontology, semantic model and\n97\tmapping of every census model. That settles what OB3 could not confirm:\n98\tquoted upper-case identifiers and `CASE WHEN` are accepted.\n99\t\n100\tIt rejected binding case V2. GND wrote every identifier quoted and\n101\tupper-cased. Ossie upper-cases a name only when it is a regular identifier\n102\tand keeps any other name as written, so `\"UNI.STUDENT\"` did not name the\n103\tdataset `uni.student`. The built-in path had the same defect for an object\n104\ttype whose id is `select` or `not`.\n105\t\n106\t**The repair.** `is_regular_identifier` in\n107\t`gnd.export.ossie_relational` states the class, measured with the pinned\n108\tvalidator over every Unicode character: a name is regular unless it is\n109\tempty, starts with an ASCII digit, holds white space or ASCII punctuation\n110\tother than `$` and `_`, or is the word `not` or `select` in any case.\n111\t`_quoted` upper-cases a regular name and writes any other as given. The\n112\trule's one home is the normative section, \"Semantic model and mapping\".\n113\t\n114\t**Held by tests.** In the default lane: a table of quoted forms, a binding\n115\twith a space, a dot and a hyphen in its names, and agreement between the\n116\texporter and the corpus checker's independent statement of the rule over\n117\tevery character. In the oracle lane: the exporter's class equals the\n118\tvalidator's over every character, and the validator accepts the\n119\tirregular-name binding and a table named `select`.\n120\t\n121\t**Approved answers changed:** `physical_binding/V2/expected.mapping.yaml`\n122\tonly. Two OB3 tests that pinned the old form (`\"SELECT\"`, and `\"A\"\"B\"` for\n123\ta name with a quote) were updated to the new one.\n124\t\n125\t## Spec obligations\n126\t\n127\t| Obligation | Discharged by |\n128\t|---|---|\n129\t| O1, O5, O9 (the pages, skill and note; OB1's flags) | the files listed in the evidence |\n130\t| O2, O7 (exits by constant; the default change) | the release note; `test_the_release_note_states_the_exits_by_their_constants` |\n131\t| O3, O6 (build and link check) | the two gates above, both exit 0 |\n132\t| O4 (one home) | `test_every_rule_id_on_a_page_is_in_the_normative_section`, `test_every_reason_on_a_page_is_one_the_code_gives`, `test_the_normative_section_names_every_rule_and_reason_in_the_code`; the reviewer's reading (F5-F7) |\n133\t| O8, O10, O11 (the logical marker, surrogate keys, the accepted limits) | the explanation and the binding how-to |\n134\t| O12 (no claim about the upstream validator) | the pages say only that GND checks against the pinned schemas; that is still true and was left |\n135\t| O13 (OB1-Q1) | to the close sweep (H8) |\n136\t| O14, O15 | the `Unreleased:` heading; no downstream name (reviewer confirmed) |\n137\t\n138\t**Normative audit (spec §5.7).** One gap: exit 64 was missing from the\n139\tsection's exit list. Added. The test above found none in rule ids, reasons\n140\tor flags.\n141\t\n142\t**T6 was discharged by reuse.** The tutorial runner already compares a\n143\tblock's shown output with the real output, so the pages use its\n144\t`output=stdout` blocks and no new matcher was written.\n145\t\n146\t## Implementation findings\n147\t\n148\t| ID | Finding | What was done |\n149\t|---|---|---|\n150\t| IF1 | The reasons `derived-computed` and `identification-unmatchable` are stated in \"Relational mapping (Rmap)\", not in \"Ossie interchange\". | The tests read both normative sections as the one home. |\n151\t| IF2 | The strict build failed on main already, on two links in `docs/agents` from another milestone. | Fixed on the owner's word (018). |\n152\t| IF3 | The refunds model does not say a refund has one amount, so the amount gets its own table. | The how-to says so, as a teaching point. |\n153\t| IF4 | The exporter's Python (3.14) and the validator's (3.11) carry different Unicode tables. | See F1 and OB5-Q1. |\n154\t\n155\t## Review\n156\t\n157\tOne Codex round, read-only, over the diff from `6758d224`\n158\t([brief](notes/reviews/phase-OB5-r1/BRIEF.md),\n159\t[review](notes/reviews/phase-OB5-r1/review.md),\n160\t[findings](notes/reviews/phase-OB5-r1/FINDINGS.md)). **One reviewer is one\n161\treviewer.** Seven findings, all reproduced, all repaired at `7a6ccfe9`:\n162\t\n163\t- **F2:** the corpus checker's normalizer disagreed with the exporter on 24\n164\t  control characters.\n165\t- **F3, F4:** the docs tests let a misspelled reason and a wrong exit number\n166\t  pass.\n167\t- **F5, F6, F7:** three false sentences on the pages.\n168\t- **F1:** a letter whose uppercase differs between Unicode versions. Stated\n169\t  as a limit, not removed (OB5-Q1).\n170\t\n171\tThe repairs are not re-reviewed.\n172\t\n173\t## Refactoring review\n174\t\n175\t**None needed.** The phase touched one product function, `_quoted`, and\n176\tadded one beside it. Both callers already went through `_quoted`, so the\n177\trule has one place in the product. The corpus checker restates it on\n178\tpurpose: it is the independent side of the comparison, and a test now holds\n179\tthe two together. The docs tests reuse `markdown_section` and the tutorial\n180\trunner. Nothing was seen and deferred.\n181\t\n182\t## Open questions\n183\t\n184\t| ID | Question | Owner |\n185\t|---|---|---|\n186\t| OB5-Q1 | F1: an identifier with a non-ASCII letter is upper-cased by the Unicode tables of the Python that runs the export. A reader on older tables may not match it. Accept as a stated limi