{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_fuK30QeppHPwF3lLOR8TwuBZ","content":"2032\t## Relational mapping (Rmap)\n2033\t\n2034\t`gnd.rmap.map_model(model)` maps a loaded model to a relational schema by\n2035\tHalpin's relational mapping procedure. The schema holds tables, typed columns\n2036\twith nullability, a primary key, unique keys and ordered foreign keys, plus a\n2037\tlist of what it could not place, each with a closed reason.\n2038\t`gnd.rmap.dispositions(model, schema)` returns exactly one row for every fact\n2039\ttype and every constraint, saying where it is carried or why it is not. The\n2040\tmapping never refuses a model the loader admits. This section is the rules'\n2041\tonly home. The worked corpus is `tests/fixtures/rmap`.\n2042\t\n2043\t**Which uniqueness counts.** Only uniqueness constraints that are both\n2044\t`confirmed` and `alethic` place fact types and form keys. Any other\n2045\tuniqueness is listed with `uniqueness-not-confirmed`, and its fact type is\n2046\tplaced as if that constraint were absent. A fact type's **effective\n2047\tuniqueness set** is built in three steps:\n2048\t1. Take its counting internal uniqueness constraints.\n2049\t2. If there are none, use one implied uniqueness spanning all roles, since a\n2050\t   fact is recorded once.\n2051\t3. Drop any uniqueness whose roles strictly include another's; it is\n2052\t   recorded as `implied`.\n2053\t\n2054\tUniqueness constraints over the same roles form one key. That key is\n2055\tpreferred if any of them is, and they all get the same disposition.\n2056\t\n2057\t**Placement.** Each fact type is placed by the first rule that applies.\n2058\t\n2059\t| Rule | Applies when | Result |\n2060\t|---|---|---|\n2061\t| 0 | `derivation: derived` with `storage` absent or `computed` | Not placed: `derived-computed`, and its constraints too |\n2062\t| 1 | A binary between an entity and the value type of its `popular` or `general` reference, with a single-role uniqueness on the value role | The entity's key column (`key`). It adds no column. |\n2063\t| 2 | Named by the external uniqueness that composes an entity's identification | Key columns of that entity's table (`key`) |\n2064\t| 3 | `objectified_as: O` | O's table. Its roles are matched to O's identifying components by the object type that plays them; matched roles are O's key (`key`), and any other role becomes a column. If O's identification cannot be resolved, O is keyed by the fact type's own primary uniqueness, and that key is O's key wherever O is referenced; the identification fact types are then placed by the later rules. |\n2065\t| 4 | Unary | A `Boolean` column named after the fact type, in its player's table. It is non-null, or nullable when absorbed from a subtype. |\n2066\t| 5 | A single-role uniqueness on role *r* | Columns in the table of *r*'s player (`columns`). The other roles become columns. |\n2067\t| 6 | Otherwise | A table named after the fact type (`table`), with one column or column group per role, keyed by its primary uniqueness |\n2068\t\n2069\t**Choosing r for a 1:1 (rule 5).** When more than one role has a single-role\n2070\tuniqueness, *r* is the one confirmed alethic mandatory role, if exactly one\n2071\texists. Otherwise the variant decides:\n2072\t- `HALPIN` takes the role whose player id sorts first, then the earlier role\n2073\t  position.\n2074\t- `ALTERNATE` takes the role whose player plays more roles with a single-role\n2075\t  uniqueness (rule-0 fact types excluded), then the player id, then the\n2076\t  position.\n2077\t\n2078\t**Which object types get a table.**\n2079\t- **An entity:** when a rule 1, 2, 3, 4 or 5 placement lands on it, or when\n2080\t  it is `independent`.\n2081\t- **A value type:** when it is the player of *r* in a rule-5 placement.\n2082\t- **Subtypes.** Under `HALPIN`, a subtype with `inherits_reference: true` is\n2083\t  absorbed: its placements land in its root supertype's table, and their\n2084\t  columns are always nullable. Under `ALTERNATE`, such a subtype gets its own\n2085\t  table, keyed by the inherited key. That key is also a foreign key to its\n2086\t  first supertype's table, which therefore always gets a table.\n2087\t- **Otherwise there is no table.** A role played by an object type without a\n2088\t  table becomes plain value columns, with no foreign key. A role played by\n2089\t  an object type that has a table, value types included, is a foreign key\n2090\t  to it.\n2091\t\n2092\t**Keys and columns.**\n2093\t\n2094\t| Identification | Key columns |\n2095\t|---|---|\n2096\t| Value type | One column named after the value type id. Its origin is `(\"reference\", id)`. |\n2097\t| `popular` or `general` reference | One column named after the reference value type id |\n2098\t| `unit-based` reference | `<id>_value`, typed `Number`. This is recognised before the general identification lookup, which does not resolve unit-based references. |\n2099\t| Composite | The **identifying** external uniqueness: `confirmed`, `alethic` and `preferred`, over binaries whose other roles are all played by the entity, each of them functional on the entity (a confirmed alethic uniqueness on the entity's role alone). If several qualify, the one with the smallest sorted role references wins, then the smaller `role_refs` sequence; declaration order never decides. An external uniqueness that references a rule-0 fact type identifies nothing. One column per component, in `role_refs` order. A value component is named after its role. An entity component is a group named `<role>_<key>`. Either kind is a foreign key when its player has a table. A composite inside a composite is not resolved. |\n2100\t| Inherited | A subtype with `inherits_reference: true` and no identification of its own is identified as its first supertype is, composite included. |\n2101\t| Unresolvable | The object type is listed as `identification-unmatchable`, and so is every fact type placed in it or needing its key, along with their constraints. |\n2102\t\n2103\t- **Primary key.** Rule 6 uses the `preferred` uniqueness if one exists.\n2104\t  Otherwise it uses the smallest one, with ties broken by the sorted\n2105\t  positions of its roles; declaration order never decides.\n2106\t- **Unique keys.** Every other effective uniqueness is a `unique_keys`\n2107\t  entry over its columns. This includes the uniqueness constraints of an\n2108\t  objectified fact type that are narrower than the key.\n2109\t- **External uniqueness.** An external uniqueness over the same roles as an\n2110\t  entity's identifying one is that entity's `primary_key`, whatever its\n2111\t  `role_refs` order. Any other is a `unique_keys` entry when all its columns\n2112\t  lie in one table. It is `spans-tables` when they do not. When a referenced\n2113\t  fact type is unmapped, the uniqueness takes that fact type's reason. Its\n2114\t  columns are in table order, each once. A unary role is held by its table's\n2115\t  key columns, not by the `Boolean` column.\n2116\t- **One key per column set.** Uniqueness constraints over the same set of\n2117\t  columns share one key and one disposition. A set equal to the primary\n2118\t  key's is the `primary_key`.\n2119\t- **Order.** Unique keys are ordered by the positions of their columns in\n2120\t  the table.\n2121\t- **Column names.** A role played by a value type gives a column named after\n2122\t  the role. A role played by an object type with key columns *k₁…kₙ* gives\n2123\t  the columns `<role>_<kᵢ>`.\n2124\t- **Column order.** Key columns come first. After them come placed columns,\n2125\t  by fact type id and then role position. An own table follows role order.\n2126\t  A name already taken in a table gets `_2`, `_3` and so on, in that order.\n2127\t- **Datatypes.** A column's `datatype` is its value type's resolved\n2128\t  primitive.\n2129\t- **Foreign keys** point at the target's primary key. They are named\n2130\t  `<from>__<to>`, then `__2` and so on, counted per pair, in column order.\n2131\t  A name is unique in the whole schema: tables are named in sorted order,\n2132\t  and a name already taken by another pair takes the next number.\n2133\t\n2134\t**Nullability.**\n2135\t- Key columns are never null.\n2136\t- Every column of a rule-6 table, and every rule-3 column, is non-null,\n2137\t  because a fact fills all its roles.\n2138\t- A rule-5 column is nullable unless a confirmed alethic mandatory covers *r*\n2139\t  and *r*'s player is not absorbed. That mandatory is then carried as\n2140\t  `not_null`.\n2141\t- **Not carried relationally** (`not-relational`, detail `mandatory`): any\n2142\t  other mandatory.\n2143\t- **Also `not-relational`, with their type as detail:** frequency, ring,\n2144\t  subset, equality, exclusion, exclusive-or, mandatory-disjunctive, value,\n2145\t  value-comparison, cardinality, subtype-partition, textual, guarded and\n2146\t  anchored constraints.\n2147\t\n2148\t**Disposition forms.**\n2149\t\n2150\t| Form | Meaning |\n2151\t|---|---|\n2152\t| `table` | A rule-6 fact type |\n2153\t| `columns` | A fact type that adds columns to an existing table |\n2154\t| `key` | A fact type that supplies key columns (rules 1 to 3) |\n2155\t| `placement` | The uniqueness that placed a rule-5 fact type |\n2156\t| `primary_key` | The uniqueness that is a key, or a uniqueness or mandatory on an identifying fact type |\n2157\t| `unique_key` | A uniqueness kept as a unique key |\n2158\t| `implied` | A uniqueness dropped from the effective set, or one on a unary |\n2159\t| `not_null` | A mandatory carried as non-null columns |\n2160\t\n2161\tUnmapped rows and dispositions are sorted by kind and then id, with ids\n2162\tcompared as strings. The mapping depends only on the model's content. It\n2163\tdoes not depend on its path, or on the order of object types, fact types or\n2164\tconstraints. The answer format the corpus uses is\n2165\t`tests/fixtures/rmap/answers/README.md`.\n2166\t\n2167\t## Ossie interchange\n2168\t\n2169\t`gnd export ossie MODEL --out FILE` projects a standalone GND model to an\n2170\tApache Ossie ontology document, version `0.3.0.dev0`, using the\n2171\t`ontology/ontology.json` schema from commit\n2172\t`ee89dbdc2844d8b7d293ed42c8613928bb99505f`. The optional parser oracle comes\n2173\tfrom `apache/ossie-converters` at\n2174\t`83ddc294c8840f5f497a2bdbec2348a128fc2254`. YAML is the default and uses 1.1-compatible scalar spelling. Select\n2175\t`--format json` for canonical JSON. The source loader refuses include-bearing\n2176\tmodels with `include:unsupported-route` before resolving their dependencies.\n2177\t\n2178\t| Source | Core projection |\n2179\t|---|---|\n2180\t| Object types and subtypes | EntityType or ValueType concepts, with subtype parents in `extends`. Safe unique names are preserved. Other names get deterministic ID-based aliases. |\n2181\t| Value primitives | The corresponding Ossie built-in, with Number projected as Decimal and Duration as Integer. The sidecar retains those source types and Binary. |\n2182\t| Popular and general references | A generated binary OneToOne relationship and an `identify_by` entry. |\n2183\t| External references | `identify_by` on functional binary relationships sharing the first-role entity. Unsupported reference details travel in the sidecar. |\n2184\t| Fact types and readings | One relationship under its first-role concept. The forward placeholders bind by source role position. Explicit inverse qualifiers bind by role name. A bare binary inverse reverses the bindings. Additional roles have explicit names. |\n2185\t| Confirmed alethic internal uniqueness | OneToOne for both directions of a binary. ManyToOne for an n−1 determinant, with the dependent role last. Reading bindings retain source order even when declaration roles move. |\n2186\t| Reviewed constraints and derivations | `requires` and `derived_by` at the scope described below. |\n2187\t| Other source rules, provenance, modality and operations | The source values travel in the sidecar. |\n2188\t\n2189\tThe formula projection supports confirmed alethic rules in these shapes.\n2190\tPlayers are String value types without subtype or supertype links, with\n2191\tan Integer value type in the compared role for the integer guard.\n2192\tA binary relationship can require an integer second-role value to be at\n2193\tmost a literal, or forbid a self-link between two roles of the same type.\n2194\tUnary relations over the same object type support subset, equality and\n2195\texclusion, with both equality directions published as one group. A\n2196\tString value concept can declare a finite enum of representable string\n2197\tliterals. Ontology scope can limit the cardinality of a named object type.\n2198\tA derived binary can use one clause with a positive chain of two asserted binaries where\n2199\tthe first last-role binding is the second first-role binding and the\n2200\tderived endpoints match the projected role order.\n2201\t\n2202\tThe loss report marks those formula pointers with `requires` and reason\n2203\t`formula`. The sidecar retains their original GND syntax and policy\n2204\tmetadata. Frequency retains its GND semantics in the sidecar because GND\n2205\tcounts repeated rows and Ossie relationships use sets. Mandatory rules\n2206\talso stay in the sidecar because GND can report vacuity for an empty\n2207\tcovered relationship. Other unverified rule shapes take the extension\n2208\tdisposition as a complete group.\n2209\t\n2210\tThe report at `FILE.loss.json` (overridable with `--loss-report`) uses\n2211\t`gnd-ossie-loss/1`. Each RFC 6901 pointer, including the root and containers,\n2212\thas one disposition and a reason from the packaged schema. A `native`\n2213\trow describes a core projection. An `extension` row carries GND-specific\n2214\tshape or value. A `refused` row names an omitted element. Nonempty population\n2215\tsubtrees are refused as `population:out-of-scope`. The report and sidecar\n2216\tomit the refused values. A successful export may contain refusals in the\n2217\treport. `--strict` rejects an export with any refusal before publication.\n2218\t\n2219\tA `redact` declaration on a fact type or a role does not change the export.\n2220\tIt withholds population values from reports, and an export carries no\n2221\tpopulation. The readings, constraints and provenance text of a redacted fact\n2222\ttype are written like any other, in every disclosure mode. When the model\n2223\tdeclares `redact`, the command prints one note on standard error with the\n2224\tnumber of fact types that declare it. The note names no fact type and no\n2225\tvalue, and the exit code stays 0.\n2226\t\n2227\tThe `FILE.gnd.json` sidecar uses `gnd-ossie-sidecar/1`. Both metadata files\n2228\tcontain the model's canonical `model_identity` and the bare hexadecimal\n2229\tSHA-256 of the exact ontology bytes, `document_sha256`. Sidecar `elements`\n2230\tgroups original source pointers by their nearest element ID. The empty\n2231\tgroup key means document scope. The entries are `object` and `array` shape\n2232\tmarkers, `copy` entries with an Ossie pointer in `from`, and `literal`\n2233\tentries with a source `value`. Literal entries preserve exact spelling\n2234\twhere the core projection uses a different representation.\n2235\t\n2236\tThe command stages all outputs, then publishes the report and sidecar\n2237\tbefore the ontology, which is the completion marker. Publication is\n2238\tcreate-once. A synchronous failure rolls back the invocation's published\n2239\tentries. An interruption before the ontology appears can leave uncommitted\n2240\tcompanions. Exit codes are 0 on success, 3 on input or strict-policy refusal,\n2241\t11 on output collision, 64 on a usage error, 66 on missing input, 70 on an\n2242\tinternal rendering failure and 74 on an I/O refusal.\n2243\t\n2244\t### Semantic model and mapping\n2245\t\n2246\tBy default the export also writes Ossie's relational layer, in the run's\n2247\tformat (`yaml` or `json`):\n2248\t\n2249\t| File | Content |\n2250\t|---|---|\n2251\t| `FILE.semantic_model.<format>` | An Ossie core-spec semantic model: the tables of the [relational mapping](#relational-mapping-rmap), under the `HALPIN` variant |\n2252\t| `FILE.mapping.<format>` | An Ossie mapping document, under the pinned `ontology/mapping.json`, from the ontology's concepts and relationships to the semantic model's datasets and fields |\n2253\t\n2254\t`--ontology-only` writes neither, and the run is then the three-file run in\n2255\tevery respect. The ontology, sidecar and loss report have the same bytes\n2256\teither way. The five files are one create-once group, published in the\n2257\torder loss report, sidecar, semantic model, mapping, ontology; a collision on\n2258\tany of the five paths exits 11 and publishes nothing. `--max-output-bytes`\n2259\tbounds each of the five. The pinned core-spec requires at least one\n2260\tdataset, so a model that maps to no table gets no semantic model and no\n2261\tmapping: the run writes the other three files, says so on standard error\n2262\tand exits 0.\n2263\t\n2264\t**The semantic model.** Its `name` is the ontology's `name` followed by\n2265\t`_relational`.\n2266\t\n2267\t| Rmap | Semantic model |\n2268\t|---|---|\n2269\t| Table | One dataset, in table order. `name` and `source` are the table name. A `GND` extension holds `{\"source_kind\":\"logical\"}`: the name is one GND chose, not a table that exists. |\n2270\t| Column | One field, in column order: `name` is the column name and `expression.sql` is that name, quoted. |\n2271\t| Primary key, unique keys | `primary_key` and `unique_keys` |\n2272\t| Foreign key | One relationship, in table order and then foreign-key order: `from` is the table that holds the key, `to` the table it references, with `from_columns` and `to_columns` in key order |\n2273\t\n2274\t- **Datatypes.** String, Integer, Float, Boolean, Date and DateTime map to\n2275\t  themselves, and Number to Decimal. Binary and Duration map to `Opaque`,\n2276\t  with the primitive in a `GND` extension on the field as\n2277\t  `{\"gnd_primitive\": …}`. A column with no primitive has no `datatype`.\n2278\t- **Identifiers in expressions** are written quoted, in Ossie's normalized\n2279\t  form, with an embedded quote doubled. A **regular** identifier, one that\n2280\t  can be written without quotes, is uppercased: `\"CUSTOMER\".\"EMAIL\"` names\n2281\t  field `email` of dataset `customer`. Ossie compares a regular identifier\n2282\t  by its uppercased form, so the quoted form names the same field and never\n2283\t  reads as a keyword. Any other name is written exactly as given, which is\n2284\t  the only form Ossie matches it by: `\"uni.student\".\"NR\"`. A name is regular\n2285\t  unless it is empty, starts with an ASCII digit, holds white space or an\n2286\t  ASCII punctuation character other than `$` and `_`, or is the word `not`\n2287\t  or `select` in any case. That is the class of the pinned Ossie validator.\n2288\t  Uppercasing follows the Unicode tables of the Python that runs the export.\n2289\t  A reader on other tables can uppercase a non-ASCII letter differently and\n2290\t  then does not match that name; a name of ASCII characters is exact.\n2291\t- **No population.** No file of the relational layer holds a row, a model\n2292\t  identity or a digest, except the mapping's extension below.\n2293\t\n2294\t**The mapping.** Its `name` is the ontology's `name` followed by `_mapping`.\n2295\t`ontology_ref` and `semantic_model_ref` each carry the referenced document's\n2296\t`name` and an `iri` of `./` and that file's name, so the three files resolve\n2297\tfrom one directory. The file name is percent-encoded, every character\n2298\toutside the unreserved set, so a `#`, `?` or `%` in it stays part of the name. Concept mappings follow the ontology's component order,\n2299\tand a concept appears only when it has an object mapping or a link mapping.\n2300\t\n2301\t- **Object mappings.** An entity type with a table of its own gets one,\n2302\t  over that table's key columns. With a one-relationship identifier it is\n2303\t  one `expression`. With a composite identifier it is `referent_mappings`,\n2304\t  one per relationship of the ontology's `identify_by`, in that order; each\n2305\t  takes its columns by the identifying fact type, never by position. A\n2306\t  value component is an `expression`. An entity component nests that\n2307\t  entity's own referent mappings, down to the `gnd_reference` relationship of\n2308\t  a simple identifier. A value type never gets an object mapping.\n2309\t- **Link mappings.** Every fact type the Rmap carries gets one tree, under\n2310\t  the concept that owns its relationship, in that concept's relationship\n2311\t  order. The tree has one node per role, in the relationship's role order.\n2312\t  Each node maps the objects of its role from the columns that hold the\n2313\t  role, and the last node names the relationship. Every node below the root\n2314\t  names its `concept`. Trees are not shared between fact types. The\n2315\t  generated `gnd_reference` relationship has no fact type and no tree.\n2316\t  A tree nests one level per role, so a fact type with more than 32 roles\n2317\t  has no tree; it keeps its table and is listed as `arity-over-limit`.\n2318\t- **NULL.** A NULL in a mapping expression yields no object and so no link.\n2319\t  Ossie does not define this; it is GND's reading. A unary fact type is a\n2320\t  Boolean flag, so its tree is one root node whose expressions are\n2321\t  `CASE WHEN <flag> THEN <key column> END`.\n2322\t- **Absorbed subtypes.** A subtype absorbed into its supertype's table has\n2323\t  no object mapping, because no column says which rows are its own. Its\n2324\t  fact types are still link-mapped, rooted at the supertype table's key.\n2325\t\n2326\t**The `GND` extension.** The mapping carries one custom extension with\n2327\t`vendor_name: GND`. Its `data` is canonical JSON, without the trailing\n2328\tnewline, under the schema id `gnd-ossie-relational/1`:\n2329\t\n2330\t| Member | Value |\n2331\t|---|---|\n2332\t| `model_identity` | The model's canonical identity. It changes with a `sample_population`; nothing else in the two files does. |\n2333\t| `ontology_sha256`, `semantic_model_sha256` | The bare hexadecimal SHA-256 of the exact ontology and semantic-model bytes of the same run |\n2334\t| `rmap_variant` | `halpin` |\n2335\t| `dispositions` | Every row of the Rmap's one walk: each fact type and each constraint, with `carried_as` or a `reason` |\n2336\t| `concepts` | Entity concepts with a table and no object mapping, each with a reason: `subtype-absorbed` or `identification-differs` |\n2337\t| `unmapped_links` | Present only when not empty: carried fact types with no link mapping, each with the reason `identification-differs` or `arity-over-limit` |\n2338\t\n2339\t`identification-differs` means the ontology does not identify an object type\n2340\tthe way its key columns do, so no object mapping for it can be written. That\n2341\tis the case for a unit-based reference, for an objectification keyed by its\n2342\town fact type, and for a subtype that inherits through several supertypes.\n2343\tEvery fact type the Rmap carries is therefore either named by exactly one\n2344\tlink tree or listed in `unmapped_links`, with one of those two reasons. `--strict` does not refuse any of\n2345\tthese: they are listed, not refused.\n2346\t\n2347\t### Physical binding\n2348\t\n2349\t`--physical-binding FILE` makes the semantic model describe tables the caller\n2350\tdelivers, and maps the ontology onto those tables. The built-in relational\n2351\tmapping is then not run. The ontology, sidecar and loss report are the bytes\n2352\tof a run without the flag. The flag cannot be combined with\n2353\t`--ontology-only` (exit 64), and a missing file exits 66.\n2354\t\n2355\tThe file is JSON, at most 10 MiB, under the schema `gnd-physical-binding/1`.\n2356\tThe packaged schema `gnd/schema/physical-binding.schema.json` defines its\n2357\tmembers and is closed at every level; this section states only what the\n2358\tmembers mean.\n2359\t\n2360\t**Tables.** This part uses only the binding.\n2361\t\n2362\t| Binding | Semantic model |\n2363\t|---|---|\n2364\t| A table | One dataset, in sorted table-name order. `name` and `source` are the table name as written, which may be schema-qualified. Its `GND` extension is `{\"source_kind\":\"physical\"}`. |\n2365\t| A column | One field, in the binding's own column order |\n2366\t| `sql_type` | `datatype`, through the map below. The raw text is kept on the field as `{\"sql_type\": …}`. |\n2367\t| `primary_key`, `unique_keys` | The dataset's keys, as given |\n2368\t| `references` | The columns of one table whose `references` name the same table and column list are **one** foreign key, and one relationship. They pair with the referenced columns in the table's column order. A table's foreign keys are ordered by their first column, and named by the relational mapping's rule. |\n2369\t\n2370\tNames are written as given, and quoted in expressions like any other: a\n2371\tregular identifier uppercased, any other name, such as the schema-qualified\n2372\t`fin.refunds`, as given. Two tables, or two columns of one table, that are\n2373\tequal after uppercasing are refused.\n2374\t\n2375\t**SQL types.** A type is words of letters, digits and underscores separated\n2376\tby spaces, with at most one parenthesized list of unsigned integers after a\n2377\tword; a word after the list needs its space too. Its name is\n2378\tthe words, casefolded and joined by one space. The map is closed:\n2379\t\n2380\t| Ossie data type | Names |\n2381\t|---|---|\n2382\t| `String` | `char`, `character`, `varchar`, `character varying`, `nchar`, `nvarchar`, `text`, `string`, `uuid` |\n2383\t| `Integer` | `smallint`, `integer`, `int`, `bigint`, `tinyint`, `int2`, `int4`, `int8`, `serial`, `bigserial`, `long`, `short`, `byte` |\n2384\t| `Decimal` | `numeric`, `decimal`, `dec`, `number` |\n2385\t| `Float` | `real`, `float`, `float4`, `float8`, `double`, `double precision` |\n2386\t| `Boolean` | `boolean`, `bool` |\n2387\t| `Date` | `date` |\n2388\t| `Time` | `time`, `time without time zone` |\n2389\t| `DateTime` | `timestamp`, `timestamp without time zone`, `timestamp_ntz`, `datetime` |\n2390\t| `DateTimeTz` | `timestamp with time zone`, `timestamptz`, `timestamp_ltz` |\n2391\t| `Opaque` | `bytea`, `binary`, `varbinary`, `blob`, `json`, `jsonb` |\n2392\t\n2393\tAny other type leaves `datatype` absent. It is never a refusal.\n2394\t\n2395\t**Roles.** `roles` gives, for a fact type and a role, the ordered columns\n2396\tthat hold the role.\n2397\t\n2398\t- **Names.** Fact type ids, object type ids, table names and column names\n2399\t  match exactly. A role name matches after Unicode compatibility\n2400\t  normalization (form KC), casefolding, and dropping every character that is\n2401\t  not a letter or a digit. This is its own rule, not the near-miss\n2402\t  normalization of the why-not report.\n2403\t- **A column** is written `{table, column}`, or as the string `table.column`.\n2404\t  The string is looked up among the declared tables and columns and never\n2405\t  split, because a table name can contain a dot.\n2406\t- **A role played by a value type** has one column. **A role played by an\n2407\t  entity type** has one column per part of that entity's identifier, in the\n2408\t  ontology's `identify_by` order, an entity part expanded in turn. An\n2409\t  identifier that nests more than 8 entities deep is not followed: that\n2410\t  entity is treated as one the ontology does not identify, so a fact type\n2411\t  through it is listed as `identification-differs` and its column count is\n2412\t  not checked.\n2413\t- **A bound fact type** has every role bound. With all its columns in one\n2414\t  table it gets a link mapping, as under the built-in mapping. Across\n2415\t  tables, it is listed as `spans-tables`.\n2416\t- **`extension_roles`** has the same shape and is checked the same way. It\n2417\t  produces no link mapping; its entries are listed in the extension.\n2418\t- **Surrogate keys.** A role held only through a generated id has no column\n2419\t  with the entity's identifying values, so it cannot be bound. Leave that\n2420\t  fact type out of `roles`; it is then listed as `unbound`. Its foreign key\n2421\t  still becomes a relationship.\n2422\t\n2423\t**Targets** are optional. `{kind: identifier, object_type}` on every column of\n2424\ta table's primary key makes the table the object type's home; the object\n2425\ttype then gets an object mapping over that key, when the key is exactly the\n2426\tcolumns its identifier is bound to there. `{kind: role, fact_type, role}`\n2427\tand `{kind: reference, object_type}` add nothing: they only have to agree\n2428\twith `roles`.\n2429\t\n2430\t**The listing.** The mapping's extension has no `rmap_variant` on a binding\n2431\trun. It gains `binding_sha256`, the bare hexadecimal SHA-256 of the binding\n2432\tfile, and `extension_roles`, the resolved entries as\n2433\t`{fact_type, role, table, columns}`.\n2434\t\n2435\t| Element | Disposition |\n2436\t|---|---|\n2437\t| A fact type bound in `roles`, in one table | `columns`, the table, then its roles' columns in role order, each once |\n2438\t| A fact type bound across tables | `spans-tables` |\n2439\t| A fact type bound only in `extension_roles` | `extension-role` |\n2440\t| Any other fact type | `unbound` |\n2441\t| A confirmed alethic uniqueness whose roles are bound in one table | `primary_key` when its columns are the table's primary key; `unique_key` when they are one of its unique keys; otherwise `not-in-binding`. An external uniqueness is placed by the columns of the roles it names, also when one of their fact types spans tables; across tables it is `spans-tables`. |\n2442\t| Any other uniqueness | `uniqueness-not-confirmed` |\n2443\t| A constraint of a fact type that is not carried | that fact type's reason |\n2444\t| Every other constraint | `not-relational`. A binding carries no nullability, so this includes `mandatory`. |\n2445\t\n2446\tWhen a table has no `unique_keys` member, each such uniqueness that is not\n2447\tthe primary key becomes a unique key of the table, one per column set. When\n2448\tthe member is present, nothing is added.\n2449\t\n2450\t**Refusals.** A refused binding exits 3 with its rule id on standard error\n2451\tand publishes nothing. The checks run in this one order, and within a rule\n2452\tthe first violation in sorted order is reported:\n2453\t\n2454\t| Step | Rule ids |\n2455\t|---|---|\n2456\t| The file | `binding:not-a-file`, `binding:size`, `binding:syntax` (not JSON, a non-finite number, nesting past the limit, a duplicate member, an integer too long to read, a string that is not Unicode text) |\n2457\t| The document | `binding:schema`, with the RFC 6901 pointer of the first violation in sorted pointer order |\n2458\t| The model's own refusals | unchanged |\n2459\t| Names | `binding:name-collision`, `binding:unknown-table`, `binding:unknown-column`, `binding:ambiguous-column`, `binding:unknown-fact-type`, `binding:role-name` (a role name with no letter or digit, or two that normalize alike, in the model or the binding), `binding:unknown-role`, `binding:unknown-object-type` |\n2460\t| Contradictions | `binding:reference-group` (a foreign key with a different number of columns than it references), `binding:reference-not-a-key` (the referenced columns are not the target's primary key or a declared unique key, as a sequence), `binding:partial-fact-type`, `binding:role-arity`, `binding:target-mismatch`, `binding:key-role` (a key column of a home table holds a role that is neither played by the home object type nor part of its identifier), `binding:incompatible-keys` (one column holds two object types that are unrelated, neither a subtype of the other nor part of the other's identifier) |\n2461\t| `--strict`, then size, then collision | unchanged |\n2462\t\n2463\t### Import a pinned ontology\n2464\t\n2465\t`gnd import ossie FILE --out MODEL` accepts an Ossie ontology in JSON or\n2466\tYAML. If `FILE.gnd.json` is present, the reader verifies the exact ontology\n2467\tSHA-256 and restores GND source pointers. It checks an adjacent\n2468\t`FILE.loss.json` against the sidecar when that report is present. Import\n2469\talways compares the restored identity with the sidecar's source identity.\n2470\tA mismatch is a refusal when the accompanying report claims a lossless\n2471\texport. When import proceeds, `source_identity_verified` states whether"}
