{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_iixK71hYG1TMVZk2QNvQHHko","content":"1\t# Ossie interchange in GND\n2\t\n3\tApache Ossie is an open source project that defines a file format for\n4\tontologies, meaning the business concepts in a domain and the relationships\n5\tbetween them. If an Ossie ontology already exists for your domain, you can\n6\timport it and use its concepts and relationships as the starting point for a\n7\tGND model. If your model lives in GND, as the refunds example does, you can\n8\texport its vocabulary and relationships so that another tool or team works\n9\tfrom the same names and connections.\n10\t\n11\tGND supports Ossie ontology version `0.3.0.dev0`. The export schema and\n12\tvalidation tool come from Apache Ossie\n13\t`ee89dbdc2844d8b7d293ed42c8613928bb99505f`. The parser used for the\n14\texport check comes from Apache Ossie Converters\n15\t`83ddc294c8840f5f497a2bdbec2348a128fc2254`.\n16\t\n17\tAn export covers three Ossie layers. The **ontology** holds concepts, their\n18\trelationships and a documented subset of constraint formulas. The **semantic\n19\tmodel** describes tables that hold the model's facts. The **mapping** says\n20\twhich table and column holds each concept and relationship. Import reads\n21\tthe ontology only. Ossie's metrics, dimensions and query interfaces are\n22\toutside this scope.\n23\t\n24\t!!! info \"Files on GitHub\"\n25\t\n26\t    [Normative mapping and file format](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/skills/gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#ossie-interchange) ·\n27\t    [Relational mapping rules](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/skills/gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#relational-mapping-rmap)\n28\t\n29\tThis page explains what the export does and why. The rules themselves have\n30\tone home, the two sections linked above.\n31\t\n32\t## Share an ontology and keep the original details\n33\t\n34\tUse `gnd export ossie MODEL --out ONTOLOGY.yaml` to share the ontology.\n35\tThe sidecar preserves GND details, and the loss report shows how each\n36\tsource field was handled. Keep the sidecar beside the ontology for a\n37\tGND round trip. `gnd import ossie ONTOLOGY.yaml --out MODEL.json` restores\n38\tthose details when the sidecar is present. What the export omitted stays\n39\tomitted: a model with example populations comes back without them. A foreign\n40\tontology imports through the supported projection and gets its own loss report.\n41\t\n42\tAn export is the model's own text. A fact type or role marked `redact` keeps\n43\tits reading, constraints and provenance text in the export, in every\n44\t[disclosure mode](../reference/disclosure.md): `redact` withholds population\n45\tvalues from reports, and an export carries no population. The command prints a\n46\tnote with the number of fact types that declare `redact`, so you can decide\n47\tbefore you share the files.\n48\t\n49\tGND keeps its `Duration` type, which counts whole days. Ossie receives an\n50\tinteger value type and the sidecar preserves its original GND meaning.\n51\tThe export report also records omissions, such as example populations.\n52\tUse `--strict` when any refusal should stop the export.\n53\t\n54\t## The tables an export describes\n55\t\n56\tA GND model states facts: a refund is requested by a customer, a refund is\n57\tfor an amount. A database stores facts in tables. The export works out a set\n58\tof tables for the model and writes them as the semantic model.\n59\t\n60\tThe method is the standard one for this kind of model:\n61\t\n62\t- A business object that has facts of its own gets a table. Its key is the\n63\t  way the business identifies it, such as a refund number.\n64\t- A fact that can hold only once per object becomes a column of that\n65\t  object's table. If each refund has at most one requesting customer, the\n66\t  customer is a column of the refund table.\n67\t- A fact that can hold many times gets a table of its own, linked back by a\n68\t  foreign key.\n69\t- A uniqueness rule that fits inside one table becomes a key of that table.\n70\t\n71\tThe result depends only on the model, so two exports of the same model give\n72\tthe same bytes.\n73\t\n74\tThese tables are GND's reading of the model, not tables anyone has built.\n75\tEach one carries the marker `\"source_kind\":\"logical\"`, so that a reader does\n76\tnot take its name for a real table in a warehouse. The semantic model and\n77\tthe mapping are written beside the ontology, as `OUT.semantic_model.<fmt>`\n78\tand `OUT.mapping.<fmt>`. Add `--ontology-only` to write the ontology, sidecar\n79\tand loss report without them.\n80\t\n81\tGND checks the semantic model and the mapping against the pinned Ossie\n82\tschemas before it writes them.\n83\t\n84\t## What is listed instead of mapped\n85\t\n86\tTables carry facts, keys and links. They do not carry every rule, and a few\n87\tshapes do not fit Ossie's mapping format. Nothing is dropped silently. The\n88\tmapping file holds a list, under its `GND` extension, of every fact type and\n89\tevery constraint in the model. Each entry says where it is carried, or gives\n90\ta reason it is not. The reasons you are most likely to meet:\n91\t\n92\t| Reason | What it means |\n93\t|---|---|\n94\t| `not-relational` | A rule over values or across facts, such as a spending limit. A table definition cannot state it. It stays in the sidecar. |\n95\t| `uniqueness-not-confirmed` | A uniqueness rule that is not both confirmed and a hard rule of the domain (alethic), so it is not written as a key. A confirmed rule that people are only expected to follow (deontic) is listed this way too. |\n96\t| `spans-tables` | A rule whose facts ended up in more than one table. |\n97\t| `derived-computed` | A fact that is worked out from others and not stored. |\n98\t| `identification-differs` | The ontology and the tables identify an object in different ways, so the link between them is not written. |\n99\t| `arity-over-limit` | A fact type with more than 32 roles. It keeps its table, and the mapping does not link it. |\n100\t\n101\tThree limits follow from the same principle of listing and not guessing:\n102\t\n103\t- A model that yields no table at all writes the ontology's three files and\n104\t  prints a note, because an Ossie semantic model needs at least one table.\n105\t- An object identified through a chain of more than 8 other objects is\n106\t  treated as one the ontology does not identify.\n107\t- A fact type with more than 32 roles is listed, as the table shows.\n108\t\n109\t## Describe the tables you delivered\n110\t\n111\tThe tables above are a proposal. If you have already built your schema, with\n112\tyour own table and column names, give the export a **binding file** with\n113\t`--physical-binding FILE`. The binding lists your tables and says which\n114\tcolumns hold which roles of the model. The semantic model then describes\n115\tyour tables, marked `\"source_kind\":\"physical\"`, and the mapping points at\n116\tyour columns.\n117\t\n118\tA binding does not need to cover the whole model. Fact types it does not\n119\tmention are listed with the reason `unbound`. A binding that contradicts the\n120\tmodel, for example by naming a role the fact type does not have, is refused\n121\twith a rule id that starts with `binding:`, and nothing is written.\n122\t\n123\tOne limit matters when you plan a binding: a role has to be bound to\n124\tcolumns that hold the model's own identifier for the object. A fact that a\n125\ttable holds only through a generated surrogate key cannot be bound. It is\n126\tleft out and listed as `unbound`, while the table and its foreign key are\n127\tstill described.\n128\t\n129\tWithout the option, an export is exactly what it was.\n130\t\n131\t- [Describe your delivered tables](../how-to/describe-delivered-tables-for-ossie.md)\n132\t\n133\t## Large models\n134\t\n135\tEach written file may be at most 10 MiB by default. A large model, such as\n136\tone composed from many shared vocabularies, can exceed that. The export then\n137\tstops with `ossie:output-size` and writes nothing. Raise the bound for that\n138\trun with `--max-output-bytes N`, a number of bytes up to 256 MiB.\n139\t\n140\tImport has the matching option. `gnd import ossie` reads documents up to\n141\t10 MiB unless `--max-input-bytes N` sets another bound.\n142\t\n143\tNeither option changes the limit on the model file itself, which stays at\n144\t10 MiB.\n145\t\n146\t## Where PyRel fits\n147\t\n148\tPyRel is optional. You can export and import using GND alone. The offline\n149\tcheck used the same Ossie Converters commit with RelationalAI 1.39.0.\n150\tIt generated PyRel source for 36 of the 38 eligible exports. Concept\n151\tnames, parent links, relationship names and identifier links matched the\n152\texported declarations. The converter refused one inherited identifier,\n153\tand the SDK could not generate source for one derivation chain. The\n154\temitted source contained no `require` calls.\n155\t\n156\tGND can generate its own PyRel rules for supported models. The formula\n157\ttest corpus compares GND checker results with hand-authored verdicts,\n158\tand the Ossie parser checks the corresponding formula syntax. Executing\n159\tOssie formulas is outside the scope of those checks.\n160\t\n161\t- [Share the refunds example](../how-to/share-an-ossie-ontology.md)\n162\t- [What PyRel is, and when you need it](what-is-pyrel.md)"}
