{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_71SoJim4F1Kfg6eAciTCijiA","content":"1\t# Share a refund model as an Ossie ontology\n2\t\n3\tYour team records who requested each refund, how much it was for, and how\n4\tit was paid. A partner wants the same vocabulary for their reporting tool.\n5\tShare the ontology so both teams use the same relationships, and the tables\n6\tthat go with it so the partner can see where each fact would be stored. Keep\n7\tthe sidecar when a GND user needs to recover the original rules.\n8\t\n9\t!!! info \"Files on GitHub\"\n10\t\n11\t    [Refunds example model](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/examples/refunds/model.orm.yaml)\n12\t\n13\tWith the `/gnd` skill, give it this prompt:\n14\t\n15\t> Export the refunds example as an Ossie ontology with its tables. Show how\n16\t> the output represents who requested a refund, and which table holds it.\n17\t> Then import it and compare the restored model with the original.\n18\t\n19\t## Export the model\n20\t\n21\tThe refunds example ships inside GND. The first command copies it into a\n22\t`refunds` folder in your working directory. The second writes the export.\n23\tUse a new output name for another run.\n24\t\n25\t<!-- gnd-run expect=0 -->\n26\t```sh\n27\tpython -c \"import gnd, pathlib, shutil; shutil.copytree(pathlib.Path(gnd.__file__).parent / 'examples/refunds', 'refunds', dirs_exist_ok=True)\"\n28\tgnd export ossie refunds/model.orm.yaml --out refunds.ossie.yaml\n29\t```\n30\t\n31\tThe export writes five files:\n32\t\n33\t<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->\n34\t```sh\n35\tls refunds.ossie.yaml*\n36\t```\n37\t\n38\t```text\n39\trefunds.ossie.yaml\n40\trefunds.ossie.yaml.gnd.json\n41\trefunds.ossie.yaml.loss.json\n42\trefunds.ossie.yaml.mapping.yaml\n43\trefunds.ossie.yaml.semantic_model.yaml\n44\t```\n45\t\n46\t| File | What it gives you |\n47\t|---|---|\n48\t| `refunds.ossie.yaml` | The ontology you can share with an Ossie reader. |\n49\t| `refunds.ossie.yaml.semantic_model.yaml` | The tables, columns, keys and links between tables that would hold the model's facts. |\n50\t| `refunds.ossie.yaml.mapping.yaml` | Which table and column holds each concept and relationship of the ontology. |\n51\t| `refunds.ossie.yaml.gnd.json` | The GND details needed to restore the original model, including the policy about refund amounts. |\n52\t| `refunds.ossie.yaml.loss.json` | A list of source fields and how the export handled each one. |\n53\t\n54\t## Read the relationship\n55\t\n56\tThe output relationship `refund_by` is many-to-one. One customer can\n57\trequest several refunds. Each refund has at most one requesting customer.\n58\t\n59\t<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->\n60\t```sh\n61\tsed -n '10,16p' refunds.ossie.yaml\n62\t```\n63\t\n64\t```text\n65\t  - name: refund_by\n66\t    verbalizes:\n67\t    - '{Refund} is requested by {Customer:customer}'\n68\t    roles:\n69\t    - concept: Customer\n70\t      name: customer\n71\t    multiplicity: ManyToOne\n72\t```\n73\t\n74\t![Each refund has at most one requesting customer.](../assets/applications/refund-requester.svg)\n75\t\n76\t## See the tables\n77\t\n78\tBecause each refund has at most one requesting customer, the customer sits\n79\tin a column of the refund's own table. The column may be empty, since the\n80\tmodel does not say every refund has a requester. The table's key is the\n81\trefund number.\n82\t\n83\t<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->\n84\t```sh\n85\tsed -n '6,18p' refunds.ossie.yaml.semantic_model.yaml\n86\t```\n87\t\n88\t```text\n89\t- name: refund\n90\t  source: refund\n91\t  primary_key:\n92\t  - refund_no\n93\t  fields:\n94\t  - name: refund_no\n95\t    expression:\n96\t      sql: '\"REFUND_NO\"'\n97\t    datatype: Integer\n98\t  - name: customer_customer_code\n99\t    expression:\n100\t      sql: '\"CUSTOMER_CUSTOMER_CODE\"'\n101\t    datatype: String\n102\t```\n103\t\n104\tThe model does not say that a refund has only one amount or one payment\n105\tchannel. So those two facts each get a table of their own, `refund_amount`\n106\tand `refund_channel`, linked back to `refund`. If your business has one\n107\tamount per refund, say so in the model and the amount moves into the refund\n108\ttable.\n109\t\n110\tThese tables are GND's reading of the model. They are not tables you have\n111\tbuilt, and each one is marked `\"source_kind\":\"logical\"` to say so. To\n112\tdescribe the tables you did build, see\n113\t[Describe your delivered tables](describe-delivered-tables-for-ossie.md).\n114\t\n115\t## Check what the tables do not carry\n116\t\n117\tTables can hold facts and keys. They cannot hold every rule. The mapping\n118\tfile lists each fact type and rule of the model with where it is carried, or\n119\tthe reason it is not. This command prints the ones that are not:\n120\t\n121\t<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->\n122\t```sh\n123\tgrep -o '\"id\":\"[a-z_/0-9]*\",\"kind\":\"[a-z_]*\",\"reason\":\"[a-z-]*\"' refunds.ossie.yaml.mapping.yaml\n124\t```\n125\t\n126\t```text\n127\t\"id\":\"constraints/0\",\"kind\":\"constraint\",\"reason\":\"not-relational\"\n128\t```\n129\t\n130\tThat one entry is the policy about refund amounts. It is a rule over values,\n131\twhich a table definition cannot express, so it travels in the sidecar. The\n132\t[Ossie explanation](../explanation/ossie.md#what-is-listed-instead-of-mapped)\n133\tdescribes the reasons you can meet.\n134\t\n135\t## Restore the model\n136\t\n137\t<!-- gnd-run expect=0 -->\n138\t```sh\n139\tgnd import ossie refunds.ossie.yaml --out restored.orm.json\n140\tgnd diff refunds/model.orm.yaml restored.orm.json\n141\t```\n142\t\n143\t| File | What it gives you |\n144\t|---|---|\n145\t| `restored.orm.json` | The restored GND model. |\n146\t| `restored.orm.json.loss.json` | The import report, including whether the restored model matched the source identity. |\n147\t\n148\tThe diff reports the same model identity for both files and compares\n149\teach of the two rules in both directions. For a GND restore, keep the\n150\tsidecar and loss report with an exact copy of the original ontology.\n151\tReformatting the ontology changes the bytes the sidecar verifies. Import\n152\treads the ontology and its two companions. It does not read the semantic\n153\tmodel or the mapping.\n154\t\n155\t## Share the ontology alone\n156\t\n157\tIf your partner wants only the vocabulary, add `--ontology-only`. The run\n158\twrites the ontology, the sidecar and the loss report, as GND did before it\n159\twrote tables.\n160\t\n161\t<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->\n162\t```sh\n163\tgnd export ossie refunds/model.orm.yaml --out vocabulary.yaml --ontology-only >/dev/null\n164\tls vocabulary.yaml*\n165\t```\n166\t\n167\t```text\n168\tvocabulary.yaml\n169\tvocabulary.yaml.gnd.json\n170\tvocabulary.yaml.loss.json\n171\t```\n172\t\n173\tAn export never overwrites a file. Running the first export again stops with\n174\texit code 11 and writes nothing, because its files already exist:\n175\t\n176\t<!-- gnd-run expect=11 -->\n177\t```sh\n178\tgnd export ossie refunds/model.orm.yaml --out refunds.ossie.yaml\n179\t```\n180\t\n181\t## Before you share another model\n182\t\n183\tCheck the loss report and the mapping's list before sharing. For example, a\n184\tmodel's example data is omitted and listed as refused. Add `--strict` to stop\n185\tthe export if any source field would be refused. A very large model can\n186\texceed the size limit of a file; the\n187\t[explanation](../explanation/ossie.md#large-models) says how to raise it.\n188\t\n189\t[Read the Ossie interchange scope](../explanation/ossie.md)."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_7pHkSUQDiierMYhfRNbq821T","content":"1\t# Describe your delivered tables in an Ossie export\n2\t\n3\tYour warehouse already has a refunds table. Its name and columns are not the\n4\tones GND would choose. You want the Ossie files to describe the table your\n5\tpartner will actually query, and to say which of its columns holds each fact\n6\tof the model.\n7\t\n8\tA **binding file** does that. It lists your tables and says which columns\n9\thold which roles. With it, the export describes your tables. Without it,\n10\tnothing about the export changes.\n11\t\n12\t!!! info \"Files on GitHub\"\n13\t\n14\t    [Example binding](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/examples/refunds/physical-binding.json) ·\n15\t    [Binding schema](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/schema/physical-binding.schema.json) ·\n16\t    [Binding rules](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/skills/gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#physical-binding)\n17\t\n18\tWith the `/gnd` skill, give it this prompt:\n19\t\n20\t> Export the refunds example as an Ossie ontology, described by the tables\n21\t> in its example binding. Show me the refunds table it writes and list\n22\t> anything in the model the binding leaves out.\n23\t\n24\t## Read the binding\n25\t\n26\tThe refunds example ships inside GND with a binding for a small warehouse.\n27\tThe first command copies the example into a `refunds` folder. The second\n28\tprints the binding.\n29\t\n30\t<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->\n31\t```sh\n32\tpython -c \"import gnd, pathlib, shutil; shutil.copytree(pathlib.Path(gnd.__file__).parent / 'examples/refunds', 'refunds', dirs_exist_ok=True)\"\n33\tcat refunds/physical-binding.json\n34\t```\n35\t\n36\t```text\n37\t{\n38\t  \"schema\": \"gnd-physical-binding/1\",\n39\t  \"tables\": {\n40\t    \"crm.customers\": {\n41\t      \"columns\": {\n42\t        \"cust_code\": {\n43\t          \"sql_type\": \"VARCHAR(12)\",\n44\t          \"targets\": [{\"kind\": \"identifier\", \"object_type\": \"customer\"}]\n45\t        }\n46\t      },\n47\t      \"primary_key\": [\"cust_code\"]\n48\t    },\n49\t    \"fin.refunds\": {\n50\t      \"columns\": {\n51\t        \"refund_id\": {\n52\t          \"sql_type\": \"BIGINT\",\n53\t          \"targets\": [{\"kind\": \"identifier\", \"object_type\": \"refund\"}]\n54\t        },\n55\t        \"cust_code\": {\n56\t          \"sql_type\": \"VARCHAR(12)\",\n57\t          \"references\": {\"table\": \"crm.customers\", \"columns\": [\"cust_code\"]}\n58\t        },\n59\t        \"amount_cents\": {\"sql_type\": \"INTEGER\"},\n60\t        \"pay_channel\": {\"sql_type\": \"VARCHAR(20)\"},\n61\t        \"loaded_at\": {\"sql_type\": \"TIMESTAMP\"}\n62\t      },\n63\t      \"primary_key\": [\"refund_id\"]\n64\t    }\n65\t  },\n66\t  \"roles\": {\n67\t    \"refund_by\": {\n68\t      \"refund\": [\"fin.refunds.refund_id\"],\n69\t      \"customer\": [\"fin.refunds.cust_code\"]\n70\t    },\n71\t    \"refund_amount\": {\n72\t      \"refund\": [\"fin.refunds.refund_id\"],\n73\t      \"amount\": [\"fin.refunds.amount_cents\"]\n74\t    }\n75\t  }\n76\t}\n77\t```\n78\t\n79\tIt has two parts:\n80\t\n81\t- **`tables`** is your schema: each table, its columns in order, their SQL\n82\t  types, the primary key, and each foreign key as a `references` entry. A\n83\t  column that holds the key of a business object says so with an\n84\t  `identifier` target. Here `refund_id` identifies a refund.\n85\t- **`roles`** connects the model to those columns. For each fact type, each\n86\t  role names the column that holds it. `refund_by` says the refund is in\n87\t  `refund_id` and the requesting customer is in `cust_code`.\n88\t\n89\tA column the model knows nothing about, such as `loaded_at`, needs no\n90\tentry in `roles`. It is still described as part of the table.\n91\t\n92\t## Export with the binding\n93\t\n94\t<!-- gnd-run expect=0 -->\n95\t```sh\n96\tgnd export ossie refunds/model.orm.yaml --out warehouse.ossie.yaml --physical-binding refunds/physical-binding.json\n97\t```\n98\t\n99\tThe semantic model now describes your tables, under your names, and the\n100\tforeign key becomes one link between them:\n101\t\n102\t<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->\n103\t```sh\n104\ttail -9 warehouse.ossie.yaml.semantic_model.yaml\n105\t```\n106\t\n107\t```text\n108\t    data: '{\"source_kind\":\"physical\"}'\n109\trelationships:\n110\t- name: fin.refunds__crm.customers\n111\t  from: fin.refunds\n112\t  to: crm.customers\n113\t  from_columns:\n114\t  - cust_code\n115\t  to_columns:\n116\t  - cust_code\n117\t```\n118\t\n119\tEach table is marked `\"source_kind\":\"physical\"`: it is a table you\n120\tdelivered, not one GND proposed. The ontology, the sidecar and the loss\n121\treport are the same files, byte for byte, as an export without the binding.\n122\t\n123\t## See what the binding leaves out\n124\t\n125\tThe binding does not have to cover the whole model. The mapping file lists\n126\twhatever it leaves out, with a reason:\n127\t\n128\t<!-- gnd-run expect=0 output=stdout -->\n129\t```sh\n130\tgrep -o '\"id\":\"[a-z_/0-9]*\",\"kind\":\"[a-z_]*\",\"reason\":\"[a-z-]*\"' warehouse.ossie.yaml.mapping.yaml\n131\t```\n132\t\n133\t```text\n134\t\"id\":\"constraints/0\",\"kind\":\"constraint\",\"reason\":\"not-relational\"\n135\t\"id\":\"refund_channel\",\"kind\":\"fact_type\",\"reason\":\"unbound\"\n136\t```\n137\t\n138\t- `refund_channel` is `unbound`: the binding names a `pay_channel` column\n139\t  but never says which fact it holds. To fix it, add this entry under\n140\t  `roles` and export again under a new name:\n141\t\n142\t    ```json\n143\t    \"refund_channel\": {\n144\t      \"refund\": [\"fin.refunds.refund_id\"],\n145\t      \"channel\": [\"fin.refunds.pay_channel\"]\n146\t    }\n147\t    ```\n148\t\n149\t- The policy about refund amounts is `not-relational`, as it is without a\n150\t  binding. No table definition can carry it.\n151\t\n152\tThe mapping also records the SHA-256 of the binding file, so a reader can\n153\ttell which binding an export was made from.\n154\t\n155\t## When a binding is refused\n156\t\n157\tGND checks the binding against the model before it writes anything. A\n158\tbinding that names something the model does not have is refused. This\n159\tcommand makes a copy with a misspelled role name and tries it:\n160\t\n161\t<!-- gnd-run expect=3 output=stderr -->\n162\t```sh\n163\tsed 's/\"customer\":/\"custmer\":/' refunds/physical-binding.json > broken.json\n164\tgnd export ossie refunds/model.orm.yaml --out broken.ossie.yaml --physical-binding broken.json\n165\t```\n166\t\n167\t```text\n168\tinvalid Ossie export input: binding:unknown-role: refund_by has no role 'custmer'\n169\t```\n170\t\n171\tThe run exits with code 3 and writes no file. The message starts with a rule\n172\tid, here `binding:unknown-role`. Fix the binding and run again. Do not\n173\tchange the model to fit a binding. The\n174\t[binding rules](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/blob/main/src/gnd/skills/gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#physical-binding)\n175\tlist every rule id and the order GND checks them in.\n176\t\n177\t## Limits\n178\t\n179\t- **Surrogate keys.** A role has to be bound to columns that hold the\n180\t  model's own identifier for the object, such as the refund number. A fact\n181\t  that a table holds only through a generated id cannot be bound. Leave it\n182\t  out of `roles` and it is listed as `unbound`. The table and its foreign\n183\t  key are still described.\n184\t- **Size.** A binding file can be at most 10 MiB.\n185\t- **Not with `--ontology-only`.** That option writes no tables, so the two\n186\t  together are a usage error.\n187\t\n188\t[Read the Ossie interchange scope](../explanation/ossie.md)."}
