{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_b8vmHlFTWebZa9N87BAWCdor","content":"docs/release-notes.md:8:## Unreleased: Ossie relational files\ndocs/release-notes.md-9-\ndocs/release-notes.md-10-- **Tables beside the ontology.** `gnd export ossie` now writes five files.\ndocs/release-notes.md-11-  The two new ones are `OUT.semantic_model.<fmt>`, the tables that hold the\ndocs/release-notes.md-12-  model's facts, and `OUT.mapping.<fmt>`, which says where each concept and\ndocs/release-notes.md-13-  relationship is stored. The ontology, sidecar and loss report keep their\ndocs/release-notes.md-14-  bytes. A file already at either new path stops the run with exit\ndocs/release-notes.md-15-  11 (`EXIT_COLLISION`) and nothing is written. `--ontology-only` keeps the\ndocs/release-notes.md-16-  three-file run. A model that yields no table writes three files and a note.\ndocs/release-notes.md-17-- **Your own tables.** `--physical-binding FILE` describes delivered tables\ndocs/release-notes.md-18-  in place of the generated ones. The file follows the packaged schema\ndocs/release-notes.md-19-  `gnd-physical-binding/1`. A binding the model contradicts is refused with\ndocs/release-notes.md-20-  a `binding:` rule id and exit 3. Together with `--ontology-only` it is a\ndocs/release-notes.md-21-  usage error, exit 64 (`EXIT_USAGE`). A fact that a table holds only\ndocs/release-notes.md-22-  through a surrogate key cannot be bound; it is listed as `unbound`.\ndocs/release-notes.md-23-- **Larger files.** `--max-output-bytes N` on `gnd export ossie` and\ndocs/release-notes.md-24-  `--max-input-bytes N` on `gnd import ossie` replace the 10 MiB bound for\ndocs/release-notes.md-25-  one run. N is a whole number of bytes, from 1 byte to 256 MiB. Any other\ndocs/release-notes.md-26-  value is a usage error, exit 64 (`EXIT_USAGE`). The model file itself\ndocs/release-notes.md-27-  stays capped at 10 MiB.\ndocs/release-notes.md-28-- **Where to read more.** [Ossie interchange in GND](explanation/ossie.md)\ndocs/release-notes.md-29-  and [Describe your delivered tables](how-to/describe-delivered-tables-for-ossie.md).\ndocs/release-notes.md-30-\ndocs/release-notes.md-31-## Unreleased: frontmatter fences\ndocs/release-notes.md-32-\ndocs/release-notes.md-33-- **An indented `---` is content.** The frontmatter reader behind experiment\ndocs/release-notes.md-34-  specs, conclusions and generated `SKILL.md` files takes a fence only at\ndocs/release-notes.md-35-  column 0, with nothing after it but spaces or tabs. An indented `---` in a\ndocs/release-notes.md-36-  block scalar or a multi-line value no longer ends the block early and drops\ndocs/release-notes.md-37-  the keys after it; a document whose only closing dashes are indented is\ndocs/release-notes.md-38-  refused as unterminated.\ndocs/release-notes.md-39-\ndocs/release-notes.md-40-## Unreleased: the admission example from a host\ndocs/release-notes.md-41-\ndocs/release-notes.md-42-- **Your own assertion entity.** The literature admission example's\ndocs/release-notes.md-43-  `admit()` takes a shape naming the assertion entity in your model and the\ndocs/release-notes.md-44-  entity types to follow from it. An assertion is identified by its own\ndocs/release-notes.md-45-  handle; every fact attached to an admitted assertion is kept, the facts of\ndocs/release-notes.md-46-  the followed entities with them, and an include root is admitted with its\ndocs/release-notes.md-47-  includes. `max_rows` bounds the rows read\ndocs/release-notes.md-48-  ([#215](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/issues/215)).\ndocs/release-notes.md-49-- **Admission rules as data.** `rules` lists the requirements a reviewed\ndocs/release-notes.md-50-  assertion must meet, in order, with a reason for each refused value. A\ndocs/release-notes.md-51-  supported assertion that fails one is `policy-refused`; the packaged\ndocs/release-notes.md-52-  comparison is now the default rules. `max_targets` (up to 1,024, the cap\ndocs/release-notes.md-53-  of `gnd judgments check`) lowers the target cap. Such a call writes\ndocs/release-notes.md-54-  `literature-assertion-admission/2`; the packaged call keeps its bytes\ndocs/release-notes.md-55-  ([#215](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/issues/215)).\ndocs/release-notes.md-56-\ndocs/release-notes.md-57-## Unreleased: the reading example from a host\ndocs/release-notes.md-58-\ndocs/release-notes.md-59-- **A live producer over your own documents.** The literature reading\ndocs/release-notes.md-60-  example's `run()` takes a producer object over a root that holds\ndocs/release-notes.md-61-  `profile.json`: your documents, your reading model and its includes, and\ndocs/release-notes.md-62-  the optional review, calibration, conflict and absence steps, each\ndocs/release-notes.md-63-  recorded `skipped` when undeclared. The run has a byte budget per document\ndocs/release-notes.md-64-  as well as in all, seven limits with ceilings, and records the exits of\ndocs/release-notes.md-65-  `verify` and `check`, 0, 1, 2 or 4. It writes\ndocs/release-notes.md-66-  `literature-reading-run/2`; the packaged run keeps its bytes\ndocs/release-notes.md-67-  ([#216](https://github.com/vasiloglou/gnd-public/issues/216)).\ndocs/release-notes.md-68-- **Replay of a host run.** `replay(folder, fixture=root)` re-derives such a"}
